            "bodyHtml": "<p>Complete the notes below. Write <strong>ONE WORD ONLY</strong> for each answer.</p><div><div>The world's oldest mechanical computer: the Antikythera mechanism</div><p>Discovery</p><ul><li>It was part of a ship's {{31}}, found in the sea near Antikythera, in Greece.</li><li>It was wrongly thought to be a piece of {{32}}.</li><li>It was later found to be a mechanism that had broken into pieces.</li></ul><p>Equipment used for analysis of the mechanism</p><ul><li>“Dome” — produces photographs which make the {{33}} clearer</li><li>“BladeRunner” — produces X-rays</li><li>originally used to identify {{34}} in engines</li></ul><p>Description</p><p>The mechanism consisted of:</p><ul><li>30 or more gear wheels made of {{35}}</li><li>models of the sun, moon and planets</li><li>a framework made of {{36}}</li></ul><p>How the mechanism was used</p><ul><li>The operator turned a {{37}} to move the gear wheels.</li><li>The sun, moon and planets could be moved into their correct positions for any date.</li><li>Most surprisingly, the mechanism could calculate when an {{38}} would occur.</li><li>It may have been used as a {{39}} when planning festivals.</li></ul><p>Later use of similar technology</p><ul><li>13th – 14th centuries: used for making {{40}} in Western Europe.</li></ul></div>",
